---
import Legal from '../layouts/Legal.astro';
import { company, supportEmail } from '../lib/site';
---

<Legal
  title="Privacy"
  description="What this website collects, and how the hosted OpenMinutes service handles audio, transcripts and account data."
  updated="24 October 2026"
>
  <h2>Who we are</h2>
  <p>
    OpenMinutes is published by <a href={company.url} rel="noopener">{company.name}</a>, {company.address}. KvK {company.kvk}, VAT {company.btw}.
    We are based in the Netherlands and the EU General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) applies. Write to
    <a href={`mailto:${supportEmail}`}>{supportEmail}</a> for anything about privacy. We have not appointed a
    data protection officer, since the law does not require one for an operation of our size.
  </p>

  <h2>In short</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>This website sets no cookies and runs no analytics, tracking or advertising.</li>
    <li>If you run OpenMinutes yourself, we receive nothing. The software has no telemetry.</li>
    <li>
      If you buy the hosted service, you decide what is recorded. We store it for you and do not use
      it for anything else.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      No cookies, no analytics, no tracking, no advertising and no third-party fonts or scripts. We
      do not use Cloudflare Web Analytics.
    </li>
    <li>
      The site is served by Cloudflare. To deliver pages and protect them from abuse, Cloudflare
      processes your IP address and request details and keeps standard server logs. We do not
      read those logs to profile visitors. Our basis is legitimate interest (GDPR art. 6(1)(f)): a
      secure, working website.
    </li>
    <li>
      The request form on the hosting page loads Cloudflare Turnstile, a bot check, when you start
      typing. Turnstile receives technical information from your browser to tell people from bots.
      It is the only third-party request the site makes, and it is not loaded until you use the form.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The hosting request form</h2>
  <p>
    When you ask for a hosted instance we receive your name (optional), email address, the plan you
    picked and your message. We use them only to answer you and to set up and bill your instance
    (basis: taking steps at your request before a contract, art. 6(1)(b), and legitimate interest in
    answering enquiries). The form sends the request to our own endpoint on Cloudflare Workers, which
    checks the Turnstile result, applies a rate limit by IP address and creates a ticket in our
    support system, Balie, which <a href={company.url} rel="noopener">Klappe Development</a> operates. Replies are sent through Emailit. We
    keep a request and its replies for as long as we need to deal with it and for up to two years after
    the last contact, unless you become a customer (see below) or ask us to delete it sooner. We do
    not sell your details or use them for marketing.
  </p>

  <h2>Email</h2>
  <p>
    If you write to us, we keep the correspondence to answer you and to have a record of what was
    agreed. Outgoing mail is delivered through Emailit.
  </p>

  <h2>The self-hosted software</h2>
  <p>
    If you run OpenMinutes yourself, we do not receive anything. The software has no telemetry, no
    crash reports, no account with us and no update ping. Your recordings, transcripts and keys stay
    on your server. You are then responsible for your own data protection duties; our
    <a href="/docs/consent-and-legal">consent notes</a> list what to check.
  </p>

  <h2>The hosted service</h2>
  <p>
    For the hosted service, you (or your organisation) are the controller of the recordings,
    transcripts and summaries and <a href={company.url} rel="noopener">{company.name}</a> acts as your processor. We process that data only
    on your instructions, which are the settings you choose and these pages. We will offer a data
    processing agreement before you start, and you can ask for one at any time at
    <a href={`mailto:${supportEmail}`}>{supportEmail}</a>. For your own account details (name, email, billing)
    we are the controller.
  </p>
  <h3>What we process for you</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Meeting audio recorded by the bot, and the participant names and chat the bot captures.</li>
    <li>Transcripts, speaker labels, summaries and minutes made from that audio, and any share links you create.</li>
    <li>Your calendar events, to find meetings to join.</li>
    <li>
      Your settings, and the provider keys, OAuth tokens and bot sign-in cookies you enter, which
      are stored encrypted.
    </li>
    <li>
      Voiceprints (numbers describing how a voice sounds), only if you switch on Learn voices.
      Checking lines against saved voices is a separate switch. Both are off by default. A
      voiceprint stores no audio. It is kept in your own database, is not sent to your
      transcription or summary providers or to any other service, and is used only to suggest a
      name or flag a doubtful line that you decide on. You can delete the voice data of one person
      or of everyone, and mark a person Do not track so no voice is ever learned or suggested for
      them. A voiceprint can be biometric data: you are responsible for having a valid basis before you
      switch it on.
    </li>
    <li>Your name and email address, to run the account.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We look at your content only when you ask us to help, or when it is needed to keep the service
    running or to investigate abuse. We do not use it to train models and we do not share it for
    advertising.
  </p>
  <h3>Retention</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      You choose how long audio is kept in the instance settings, and you can delete any meeting
      yourself. Deleting a meeting removes its audio, transcript and summary.
    </li>
    <li>
      When your subscription ends we delete the instance and its data within 30 days after the end
      of the paid month, after you have had the chance to export it. Backups of it are
      removed within a further 30 days.
    </li>
    <li>
      Invoices and payment records are kept for 7 years, as Dutch tax law requires.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Where it runs</h3>
  <p>
    Hosted instances run on infrastructure operated by <a href={company.url} rel="noopener">Klappe Development</a> in the Netherlands.
    Your data stays there, apart from what you send to providers you choose yourself (below).
  </p>
  <h3>Security</h3>
  <p>
    Instances are served over HTTPS. Provider keys, tokens and bot sign-in cookies are encrypted
    at rest. Access to the servers is limited to us. If a breach affects your
    data we will tell you without undue delay, so that you can meet your own duty to report it.
  </p>

  <h2>Who else touches your data</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Cloudflare</strong>: website hosting, DNS, the form endpoint and the Turnstile bot
      check. Cloudflare is a US company and may process data outside the EU under the EU-US Data
      Privacy Framework and standard contractual clauses.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Emailit</strong>: delivery of our email, including replies to your request.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your own AI providers</strong>: if you enter a key for OpenAI, DeepSeek, Groq,
      Deepgram, AssemblyAI, Anthropic or another provider, the audio or text you send goes to that
      provider under your agreement with them. We do not choose or resell one. Local Whisper sends
      nothing out. Check where your provider processes data.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Google and Microsoft</strong>: your calendar data is read through the apps you create,
      and the bot joins Google Meet and Microsoft Teams meetings through their services.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>We do not sell personal data.</p>

  <h2>Recording other people</h2>
  <p>
    The bot joins under a visible name and can post a notice in the meeting chat. You must tell
    participants and follow the consent rules that apply to your meetings. Our
    <a href="/docs/consent-and-legal">consent notes</a> list what to check.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    You can ask for access, correction, export or deletion of your data, restrict or object to how
    we use it, and receive it in a portable format. Write to
    <a href={`mailto:${supportEmail}`}>{supportEmail}</a>; we answer within one month. You can also complain
    to the Dutch Data Protection Authority (Autoriteit Persoonsgegevens, autoriteitpersoonsgegevens.nl).
    Participants who were recorded should contact the person who ran the meeting first, since they
    control that data. If they contact us, we pass the request on to that person.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If we change this page in a way that matters, we update the date above, and tell hosted customers
    by email.
  </p>
</Legal>
